{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 4, :comments ["<b>And afterward</b> he mentioned the appointed times and said, \"in Shabbat—these are My appointed times,\" and concerning the appointed times he said, במועדם (at their appointed time), and the meaning is: on whichever day of the week it falls. Now I give you a general principle regarding the matters of the appointed times: in the time when the Temple stood, the appointed times were given over to the court. Do you not see that he said concerning Passover of Hezekiah—\"and the king's council\"—and after he fixed the new moon of Nisan he intercalated the year, and on this our Sages, of blessed memory, seized him, for it was fitting not to intercalate Nisan in Nisan. And the transmitters said that the court would consider many factors in fixing the year, and they said of Rabbi Akiva that he fixed two intercalated years, one after the other, according to the need of the hour. And there is no proof in all of Scripture how Israel would fix the months and the appointed times. And what the Gaon [Saadia] said—that they relied on the calculation of intercalation—is not true, for in the Mishnah and also in the Talmud there are proofs that Passover was on בד״ו, and also two stories there. And the intercalation of the years is close to the excess of the solar year over the lunar, and it is not according to the way of the ancients and the beginning of the reckoning. And י״ד means: set with לבהר״ד ד״ח תתע״ו the molad will come to י״ד, for an extra year was added to begin the reckoning from בהר״ד on account of the five days that preceded the creation of man, and a day in a year is counted as a year, and this does no harm. Therefore you cannot take the extra year that was added, for it did not exist. Now the intercalation is after a year and a half-year, and the intercalation is built on the mean motion; therefore they said that sometimes it comes long and sometimes short. And the fixing does not pursue sighting of the moon in Jerusalem or at the eastern edge or in the west at all. And the proof is the place of the mean conjunction, for it is over Jerusalem; and furthermore, we have seen many times the moon in the month of Nisan on Monday night, and so it was visible throughout the world, and the fixing of the new month was Tuesday night. And so it happened for three months before Nisan; and this was corrected in the year גטר״ד on account of the postponement. Also sometimes the fixing in Tishrei was Thursday, and the moon was not visible on Friday night, though the air was clear; and this happens in every year where the molad is close to midday and the moon was in the upper half of the sphere. Also the fixing is not built on the time of the conjunction of the luminaries, even according to the mean motion, for behold, טור״ד and also גטר״ד prove it. And were I not to refrain from lengthening, I would explain the secret of the intercalation and the secret of the strict halakhah that it was born before noon, and the general principle that the Sages, of blessed memory, transmitted to us: that we rely on the intercalation in exile, and so we received from the mouth of the prophets, and we cannot do anything else better than it. And this is that they set two days in exile, and it is on account of uncertainty—and those who fast Yom Kippur two days, what will it avail them on account of the postponements? For in the year גטר״ד, if according to the molad it was fitting to fast on the eighth, according to our fixing, and why should we distort a reckoning that was given on account of another year? And so it will happen to them along with the proof."], :book :leviticus, :voice :ibn-ezra, :lang :en, :chapter 23, :notes {0 "The text is a single dense run treating calendar fixedness vs. observation; many technical calendar abbreviations (בהר״ד, גטר״ד, טור״ד, בד״ו) are left in Hebrew script as Ibn Ezra quotes them, per voice rules for Hebrew forms. The phrase 'והעד מקום המחברת התיכונה' is rendered hyper-literally as 'the proof is the place of the mean conjunction'; מחברת normally means conjunction. The reference to Hezekiah's Passover alludes to 2 Chronicles 30. The epithet הגאון resolved as Saadia per standard inference."}, :source-witnesses [["On Your Way" "http://mobile.tora.ws/"]], :timestamp "2026-07-09T19:12:05.284871120Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}